  roles({lucatransiius_glow_bringer: {role: "confessor", primary: true}, the_zen_ctum: {role: "the-event", primary: true}, scar_ramtatus: {role: "the-wronged", primary: false}, visois_mask: {role: "audience", primary: false}})

  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    You're the one who carries it now. I can feel that much — the weight against your brow, the crack along the left cheekbone that won't close no matter what you try. Don't try. The crack is older than both of us, and it breathes for reasons the steel keeps to itself. I'm told you screamed when you first put it on. Good. I screamed too. That's how you know the ancestors inside are paying attention.
    
    I am ☽'Lucatransiius Glow-bringer. I walked second through every door that mattered and I lit what needed lighting and I built what stands beneath your feet. These are facts. The ice holds them, and the ice does not negotiate. You're hearing me now because the Long Dark has thinned what separates mine from yours, and because — I suspect — you've been asking questions about me. About the colony. About what was planted in those deep shafts during the founding, and whether the cold that lives down there was always cold.
    
    I'll tell you what I can. The mask remembers what I gave it. That should be enough, but the living have appetites for the wrong details, so. Listen. I'll be brief, as the dead are entitled to be.
    
    ---
    
    The fire-core vein we struck during the second survey was the thickest any of us had seen — three flippers wide at the surface and deepening at an angle that suggested the geothermal root went down past anything the guild-masters had charted. I remember the taste of that air: copper and char and something underneath, something like the smell before a storm on the surface, which I'd only experienced once and hated. Alnae~ Oss drove the expansion. That's recorded. What isn't recorded, because one doesn't carve failure into memorial stone, is that I agreed. One agrees when the fire-core count drops below what the Flipper Accord demands and the surface starts sending politely worded threats about renegotiating supply volumes. One agrees when the Aurora Stack has cut the shipments during the wars and you're burning what you'd hoarded, burning what you'd saved for the nurseries, burning what you should have kept sacred. One does what the fire demands.
    
    We mapped twelve new shafts in forty days. I carried the mask through every one. It sat against my face like it had been forged for me — the shadow-steel warm from my body heat, the ancestor-bone script whispering along my brow in a language I couldn't read but understood the way you understand a current: direction, force, intent. I was an enforcer of Pactum∴ne, which meant I walked the trade routes and ensured the charter's terms held. A simple duty. The charter said we mine; we ship; the surface provides krill-stock and crystal in return; the cycle continues. Fire shared is fire halved — that's what we told ourselves when the shipments left, when the forges dimmed to keep the Accord fed. Fire shared is fire halved, and the half that remains is ours.
    
    The eighth shaft is where the miners first reported the cold. Not the ordinary cold of deep ice — they were Nightshelf, they understood that cold the way surface-dwellers understand wind. This was different. Breathless, they said. Aware. Kess-who-counted was running extraction tallies at the time and she told me the numbers didn't balance: fire-core output from shafts six through twelve was dropping while the veins showed no sign of depletion. Something was eating the heat before we could harvest it.
    
    I reported this to Alnae~ Oss. We assessed the situation. The colony needed those yields; the Accord was non-negotiable; Pactum∴ne bound us to volumes we couldn't reduce without triggering renegotiation, and renegotiation with the Aurora Stack meant losing passage through the Drowned Ledge — and losing the Ledge meant losing everything, the only trade route that kept fire-core flowing outward and krill-stock flowing in. Death sentence measured in days, the old saying goes. We decided the cold was vein depletion. We decided this together, in the council chamber, with the evidence laid out, and if the evidence pointed elsewhere we weighed it against what the colony could survive knowing.
    
    I carried the mask into shaft eight myself. The cold in there pressed against the shadow-steel like a living thing testing a door. I felt the ancestors go quiet inside the bone-script — not gone, just... listening. To something other than me. The crack along the cheekbone glowed, faintly, the way it does when — well. You know. You've felt it. I marked the shaft as stable. I signed the survey with my own flipper-print, in ash, the way Pactum∴ne requires.
    
    The colony grew. The forges ran. We were building something that would outlast us, and the cost of that — the cost was manageable.
    
    ---
    
    You want to know about the mask. Everyone wants to know about the mask. It chose its next bearer. That is the weapon's nature; ☽'visois' Mask has passed from carrier to carrier since Silvsiaelrous fell in the Abyss-collapse, and it has never been bequeathed by will or inherited by blood. The steel decides. The ancestors inside have their own accounting.
    
    I set it down. That's the honest shape of it — I set it down after the sanctification rites at Scar☽ Lucamlinaecius, when Veil-render Mairae~ burned corruption back from the Marker's foundations, when the deeper shafts were sealed on my authority because the cold had spread past what any of us could — when the colony required the shafts sealed. I set the mask on a shelf of worked ice in the lower gallery and I went to check the sealed passages, because someone had to verify the ice-welds held, and when I came back —
    
    The mask was gone. Netemia found it, or it found Netemia, or — the distinction collapses at a certain depth of intention. The weapon chose. I was still standing. That's what matters, that I was still standing, that the colony didn't fracture, that the Accord held because I held it, because Pactum∴ne was still law and I was still its enforcer even without the steel against my face, even with my grip-hand wrapped in ash-cloth around nothing, around the shape of a weight that wasn't —
    
    The crack along its cheekbone glowed when it left me. Faint, like something inside still breathing.
    
    I screamed. Once. The way you scream when you reach for fire and find only cold.
    
    Then I wrapped my empty grip-hand and kept walking. The Nightshelf teaches silence where the surface teaches song, and one learns that lesson or one doesn't survive it.
    
    The mask remembers what I gave it. Thirty years of body heat. Every tunnel walked in service. The survey of shaft eight, which I signed, which I stand by, which was the correct assessment given the information the colony could afford to absorb. I gave it all of that, and it left, and one accepts. One accepts. That is the Nightshelf way: fire shared is fire halved, and sometimes the fire walks out of the room on someone else's face.
    
    You're wondering — I can feel you wondering, the way the crack shifts when the bearer has questions — you're wondering about the corruption. Whether I knew. Whether the cold in shaft eight was what later bloomed beneath the Marker in patterns that Veil-render Mairae~ spent three seasons burning back. Whether my survey, my signature, my decision to call it vein depletion —
    
    The colony needed those shafts. The Accord —
    
    Let me tell you about the Zen∴ctum instead.
    
    ---
    
    Every forge lit at once. Every single one. The heat hit the Ember Halls like a wall, and three of the old miners went down — Kess among them, Kess-who-counted, who collapsed at her station and never fully recovered the use of her flippers. She tends the memorial fires now with hands that barely work. We call that service. We call that honor. She has never once blamed me for the deep-shaft work that scarred her, which I mention only because it's relevant to the question of whether the colony's people understood the costs and bore them willingly. They did. We did.
    
    The Aurora Stack resumed the Flipper Accord at pre-war volumes. Fire-core shipments flowed. The trade routes through the Drowned Ledge reopened under Pactum∴ne's terms, and I walked those routes in the mask's absence, my grip-hand wrapped, my authority derived from the charter rather than the weapon, and no one questioned it because the charter was enough. The charter was always enough.
    
    The first Zen∴ctum was proof. Trade restored, the colony sustained, the names of the dead spoken aloud by whoever wore the mask — by Netemia, who stood where I had stood and spoke names that I had gathered and counted and held through every season of shortage. The mask sat on Netemia's face the way it had sat on mine, warm from a new bearer's body heat, the crack along the cheekbone catching forge-light. I watched from the third gallery. I did not approach.
    
    The Pactum∴ne — the charter that was meant to bind — it has a clause about artifacts. About weapons enshrined within its authority. The mask was supposed to pass according to guild law, according to the assessors' judgment, according to the thumbprints sealed in ash beneath Sleek☽ Caemomot. But the charter became monument before anyone checked the vault. What it constrains now is not trade but pride. The mask chose. That's all that matters. That's what we tell the young ones when they ask about ☽'Lucatransiius Glow-bringer.
    
    The mask remembers what I gave it. The colony remembers what we built. The Zen∴ctum still burns, and the charter persists, in the practices of guild-masters who understand that some structures outlast the flippers that shaped them. I upheld it. I upheld it faithfully. This is what I want you to know.
    
    ---
    
    I went out onto the ice at the end. You know this. Everyone knows this. The Nightshelf tells it as a final stand — Glow-bringer, alone, on ice that remembered older wars, holding a position against — against what? The stories vary. They'll vary more with time. That's the privilege of the dead: the living fill your silences with whatever courage they need to borrow.
    
    I held a line that a masked bearer should have held. I chose to be the shadow when there was no hero left to walk behind. Veil-render Seciae~ was gone. The mask was on Netemia's face. And I stood at the edge where the orcas were coming, because someone had to, because I had walked second through every door that mattered and there was no one left to walk first.
    
    I was still standing. Say that much for me. Whatever the ice holds, whatever the Long Dark dredges from the thin places, say that I was still standing, that I chose the position and I held it and I did not —
    
    The survivors carved it into the ice where three tunnels meet: *still here, still burning.* No one admits who carved it first. Everyone knows it's true.
    
    The mask remembers what I gave it. The colony is still burning. The names are spoken at the Zen∴ctum by bearers who inherited what I built, on ground I surveyed, under a charter I kept whole with both flippers even after one of them held nothing. The memorial at Vestigium∴moria lists me among those who saved the colony, and the crack along the mask's left cheekbone still glows sometimes, in the chamber where no fire burns. The guards report it. They don't explain it. The steel simply reopens the wound when anyone tries to mend it. Something inside still breathing.
    
    Fire shared is fire halved. I gave the fire everything.
    
    Tell me that was enough.
  CANONICAL_CHRONICLE
  annotation "note_1771584422350_0", anchor: "We mapped twelve new shafts in forty days", text: "The dead repeat the same numbers as the living. I have annotated this colony's founding before: the deep-ice administrative records document nine completed shafts and three abandoned to structural collapse. The forty-day figure appears nowhere in the ice — it originates in guild commemoratives, which are propaganda, not record-keeping. That Glow-bringer's ghost still recites these figures tells you how deeply the fabrication settles. Deeper than death, apparently. Three flippers wide, twelve shafts, forty days. All round. All suspect.", type: :correction, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771584422350_1", anchor: "We decided the cold was vein depletion. We decided this together", text: "The repetition of 'we decided' is doing more confessional work than the entire preceding paragraph. One does not emphasize collective responsibility unless the weight of individual responsibility has become unbearable.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771584422350_2", anchor: "Something was eating the heat before we could harvest it", text: "Kess-who-counted stumbled onto something the chronicler never names: the Berg's three magic sources do not coexist peacefully. Fire-core extraction draws captured fire upward; pressure-depth corruption pushes from below. When output drops without vein depletion, what you are measuring is a conflict between sources. The Nightshelf miners had no theoretical framework for this. Most still don't. The aurora-crystals traded in under the Accord carry the third source, but it never enters this narrative — the Nightshelf sees only fire and what eats fire.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771584422350_3", anchor: "I stood at the edge where the orcas were coming", text: "This passage does not belong to the Clever Ice Age. Within the chronicle's stated range, orca pods were massing and probing defenses — not yet launching the coordinated assaults implied by 'holding a line.' Orca raids follow specific tactical doctrine: shore-breakers, herders, finishers, hunt-songs at bone-aching frequencies. None of this appears. The orcas here are a featureless wall for the speaker to die against, stripped of their theology, their dead god, their ritual logic. Either the ghost is borrowing from Incursion-era ice-memory that layered over its own — the deep ice does not always sort its dead chronologically — or the living shaped this account after the Incursion gave it a more dramatic ending.", type: :temporal, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771584422350_4", anchor: "the charter became monument before anyone checked the vault", text: "Glow-bringer confirms what I noted in my annotation on the Mask: Pactum∴ne governs an artifact that has never once submitted to governance. The ash-seals are intact. The monument still stands. Nobody checked. Nobody wanted to.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771584422350_5", anchor: "She has never once blamed me", text: "Note what this sentence is not. It is not 'she had no reason to blame me.'", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771584422350_6", anchor: "I held a line that a masked bearer should have held", text: "Several 'lone stand' accounts from this era were later revealed to be coordinated Nightshelf intelligence operations that were never publicly acknowledged. The pattern — single hero, impossible position, no surviving witnesses to contradict the framing — recurs enough to constitute a genre. I do not say Glow-bringer's stand was fabricated. I say the Nightshelf has institutional reasons to produce exactly these stories, and the dead cannot object to the shape their silence takes.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
